{
  "title": "Inheritance and composition",
  "summary": "Extend a class, call super(), or build from parts instead.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Inheritance",
      "body": [
        "```python",
        "class Animal:",
        "    def __init__(self, name):",
        "        self.name = name",
        "    def speak(self):",
        "        return \"...\"",
        "    def intro(self):",
        "        return f\"{self.name} says {self.speak()}\"",
        "",
        "class Dog(Animal):                 # C#: class Dog : Animal",
        "    def __init__(self, name, breed):",
        "        super().__init__(name)     # C#: : base(name)",
        "        self.breed = breed",
        "    def speak(self):               # every method is \"virtual\"",
        "        return \"woof\"",
        "```",
        "",
        "There is no `virtual`/`override`: any method can be replaced in a subclass. `isinstance(d, Animal)` is C#'s `d is Animal`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "class Animal:",
        "    def __init__(self, name):",
        "        self.name = name",
        "    def speak(self):",
        "        return \"...\"",
        "    def intro(self):",
        "        return f\"{self.name} says {self.speak()}\"",
        "",
        "class Cat(Animal):",
        "    def speak(self):",
        "        return \"meow\"",
        "",
        "class Kitten(Cat):",
        "    def speak(self):",
        "        return super().speak() + \"!\"",
        "",
        "for a in [Animal(\"x\"), Cat(\"tom\"), Kitten(\"bit\")]:",
        "    print(a.intro())",
        "print(isinstance(Kitten(\"k\"), Animal))"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "x says ...",
        "tom says meow",
        "bit says meow!",
        "True"
      ],
      "explain": "`intro` lives in `Animal` but `self.speak()` calls the most specific override. `super().speak()` reaches the parent's version."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "A subclass defines `__init__` but forgets `super().__init__(name)`. What happens?",
      "options": [
        "The parent's `__init__` never runs, so `self.name` does not exist",
        "Python calls the parent automatically",
        "A compile error",
        "The subclass cannot be created"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Unlike C# constructors, Python does not chain `__init__` for you. Forgetting `super().__init__` is a common source of `AttributeError`."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Abstract base classes",
      "body": [
        "`abc` gives you C#-style abstract classes:",
        "",
        "```python fragment",
        "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod",
        "",
        "class Shape(ABC):",
        "    @abstractmethod",
        "    def area(self): ...",
        "",
        "    def describe(self):",
        "        return f\"{type(self).__name__} with area {self.area():.1f}\"",
        "",
        "Shape()   # TypeError: can't instantiate abstract class",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "sort",
      "prompt": "Inheritance (is a) or composition (has a)?",
      "buckets": [
        "Inheritance",
        "Composition"
      ],
      "items": [
        [
          "a `Manager` is an `Employee`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "a `Car` has an `Engine`",
          1
        ],
        [
          "a `Report` uses a `Formatter`",
          1
        ],
