          "a `Square` is a `Shape`",
          0
        ],
        [
          "an `Order` has `OrderLine`s",
          1
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Composition over inheritance",
      "body": [
        "Inheritance says \"is a\". Often what you want is \"has a\": build an object out of other objects and **delegate** to them.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "class Engine:",
        "    def start(self):",
        "        return \"vroom\"",
        "",
        "class Car:",
        "    def __init__(self, engine):",
        "        self.engine = engine          # has an engine",
        "    def start(self):",
        "        return self.engine.start()    # delegate",
        "```",
        "",
        "Composition keeps classes small, lets you swap parts (an `ElectricEngine`), and makes testing easy (pass a fake). Deep inheritance trees are rare in good Python."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "order",
      "prompt": "Arrange the subclass so it extends `Employee` and adds a team.",
      "lines": [
        "class Manager(Employee):",
        "    def __init__(self, name, salary, team):",
        "        super().__init__(name, salary)",
        "        self.team = team"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Shapes",
      "prompt": [
        "Write an abstract class `Shape` (using `abc`) with an abstract method `area()` and a normal method `describe()` returning e.g. `\"Circle area 3.14\"` (class name, then area to 2 decimals).",
        "",
        "Then write `Rectangle(w, h)`, `Square(side)` (a subclass of `Rectangle`, calling `super().__init__`) and `Circle(r)` (use `math.pi`)."
      ],
      "starter": "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nimport math\n\nclass Shape(ABC):\n    ...\n",
      "solution": [
        "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod",
        "import math",
        "",
        "class Shape(ABC):",
        "    @abstractmethod",
        "    def area(self):",
        "        ...",
        "",
        "    def describe(self):",
        "        return f\"{type(self).__name__} area {self.area():.2f}\"",
        "",
        "class Rectangle(Shape):",
        "    def __init__(self, w, h):",
        "        self.w = w",
        "        self.h = h",
        "",
        "    def area(self):",
        "        return self.w * self.h",
        "",
        "class Square(Rectangle):",
        "    def __init__(self, side):",
        "        super().__init__(side, side)",
        "",
        "class Circle(Shape):",
        "    def __init__(self, r):",
        "        self.r = r",
        "",
        "    def area(self):",
        "        return math.pi * self.r ** 2"
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "Dojo.throws(\"Shape cannot be created\", TypeError, lambda: Shape())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"rectangle area\", 6, Rectangle(2, 3).area())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"square area\", 16, Square(4).area())",
        "Dojo.check(\"a Square is a Rectangle\", isinstance(Square(1), Rectangle), \"class Square(Rectangle)\")",
        "Dojo.eq(\"circle describe\", \"Circle area 3.14\", Circle(1).describe())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"square describe\", \"Square area 9.00\", Square(3).describe())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"total area of a list of shapes\", 10.0, round(sum(s.area() for s in [Square(2), Rectangle(2, 3)]), 1))"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`@abstractmethod` on `area` in `Shape(ABC)`.",
        "`type(self).__name__` gives the subclass name.",
        "`Square.__init__` calls `super().__init__(side, side)`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Compose a logger",
      "prompt": [
        "Write a class `Notifier` that **has** a list of senders. Each sender is any object with a `send(message)` method.",
        "",
        "- `Notifier(senders)` stores them",
        "- `notify(message)` calls `send` on each and returns how many senders it called",
        "- `add(sender)` adds one more",
        "",
        "Also write `ConsoleSender` whose `send` prints `console: MESSAGE`, and `MemorySender` whose `send` appends to its own `sent` list."
      ],
      "starter": "class ConsoleSender:\n    ...\n\nclass MemorySender:\n    ...\n\nclass Notifier:\n    ...\n",
      "solution": [
        "class ConsoleSender:",
        "    def send(self, message):",
        "        print(f\"console: {message}\")",
        "",
        "class MemorySender:",
        "    def __init__(self):",
        "        self.sent = []",
        "",
        "    def send(self, message):",
        "        self.sent.append(message)",
        "",
        "class Notifier:",
        "    def __init__(self, senders=None):",
        "        self.senders = list(senders or [])",
        "",
        "    def add(self, sender):",
        "        self.senders.append(sender)",
        "",
        "    def notify(self, message):",
        "        for s in self.senders:",
        "            s.send(message)",
        "        return len(self.senders)"
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "mem = MemorySender()",
        "n = Notifier([mem])",
        "Dojo.eq(\"notify returns the number of senders\", 1, n.notify(\"hi\"))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"memory sender stores messages\", [\"hi\"], mem.sent)",
        "n.add(ConsoleSender())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"console sender prints\", \"console: up\\n\", Dojo.capture(lambda: n.notify(\"up\")))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"both received it\", [\"hi\", \"up\"], mem.sent)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"separate memory senders\", [], MemorySender().sent)"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`Notifier` loops over `self.senders` and calls `s.send(message)`.",
        "Give `MemorySender` its own list in `__init__`."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
